trix and $C$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $B$ and $3$ rows in $C,$ so $B$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={BC}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $BC,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-10=(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -14\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-14=(-3)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$BC=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -14\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Matrices When They Are Conformable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100741 :question/id #uuid "c3ed8d05-758d-5ae0-bf57-3779fde6020b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5885" :question/problem "Compute the product \n $\\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -1 \\\n \t0 \\\n        -2\n \t\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -5\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \n\t\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -1 \\\n \t0 \\\n        -2\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -5\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n\n    \\